      "text": "Let $x = 2.$  Then\n\\[f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16.\\]Let $x = \\frac{1}{2}.$  Then\n\\[f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = \\frac{1}{4}.\\]Multiply the second equation by $2,$ square both equation, and subtract.  This gives\n\\[(f(2) - 2 f(2))^2 - \\left( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) \\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{16} - 16^2.\\]In other words,\n\\[f^2(2) - 4 f(2)^2 + 4 f^2(2) = \\frac{1}{16} - 256,\\]or\n\\[5 f^2(2) - 4 f(2) - 417 = 0.\\]Hence,\n\\[f(2) = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 32 \\cdot 417}}{10} = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{12897}}{5}.\\]Of those two solutions, it must be the case that\n\\[f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{16} > 0.\\]In other words, $f(2) > 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right),$ which is consistent with $f(2) = \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{12897}}{5}.$  Thus,\n\\[f(2) = \\boxed{\\frac{2 + \\sqrt{12897}}{5}}.\\]The answer is: \\frac{2+\\sqrt{12897}}{5}",
